1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,347
Two friends share two sweets.

2
00:00:02,347 --> 00:00:07,089
When they split up, each can take one
sweet, or one friend can grab both.

3
00:00:07,089 --> 00:00:11,480
A snapped biscuit can break into
equal halves, or one side keeps more.

4
00:00:11,880 --> 00:00:14,268
How you split decides
who ends up with what.

5
00:00:14,268 --> 00:00:15,746
Bonds split like this too.

6
00:00:16,196 --> 00:00:19,740
Aarav learnt that a covalent bond
is a pair of electrons shared by two

7
00:00:19,740 --> 00:00:20,640
atoms.

8
00:00:20,583 --> 00:00:24,095
So in the corridor he asks Diya:
when a bond breaks, who keeps the two

9
00:00:24,095 --> 00:00:24,995
electrons?

10
00:00:25,207 --> 00:00:29,478
By the end, you will know both ways
a bond breaks, and who attacks whom

11
00:00:29,478 --> 00:00:30,505
in a reaction.

12
00:00:31,155 --> 00:00:34,599
First, breaking a bond is called
bond fission, or cleavage.

13
00:00:34,599 --> 00:00:36,381
There are two ways to do it.

14
00:00:36,731 --> 00:00:39,574
In homolytic fission, the split is fair.

15
00:00:39,574 --> 00:00:41,626
Each atom takes one electron.

16
00:00:41,626 --> 00:00:42,888
Homo means same.

17
00:00:43,238 --> 00:00:47,345
An atom left with one unpaired
electron is called a free radical.

18
00:00:47,345 --> 00:00:49,457
It is shown with a single dot.

19
00:00:49,807 --> 00:00:52,334
In heterolytic fission,
the split is unfair.

20
00:00:52,334 --> 00:00:54,078
One atom takes both electrons.

21
00:00:54,078 --> 00:00:55,422
Hetero means different.

22
00:00:55,772 --> 00:00:57,993
The atom that takes the
pair becomes negative.

23
00:00:57,993 --> 00:00:59,949
The atom that loses it becomes positive.

24
00:00:59,949 --> 00:01:01,598
So heterolytic fission makes ions.

25
00:01:02,248 --> 00:01:04,773
Let's watch it on our molecule bench.

26
00:01:04,773 --> 00:01:06,920
Here is tertiary butyl bromide.

27
00:01:06,920 --> 00:01:10,601
A carbon bonded to three
methyl groups and one bromine.

28
00:01:10,951 --> 00:01:13,274
Bromine pulls the shared pair harder.

29
00:01:13,274 --> 00:01:14,822
Watch the curved arrow.

30
00:01:14,822 --> 00:01:17,844
It starts at the bond
pair and ends on bromine.

31
00:01:18,244 --> 00:01:20,314
The pair moves onto bromine.

32
00:01:20,314 --> 00:01:21,620
The bond breaks.

33
00:01:21,620 --> 00:01:25,441
Bromine leaves as a bromide
ion, with a minus charge.

34
00:01:25,841 --> 00:01:30,360
The carbon is left with only six
electrons around it, and a plus charge.

35
00:01:30,360 --> 00:01:31,860
This is a carbocation.

36
00:01:31,860 --> 00:01:34,549
It is flat, and its carbon is s p two.

37
00:01:34,949 --> 00:01:36,210
Now a fair split.

38
00:01:36,210 --> 00:01:38,233
A chlorine molecule in sunlight.

39
00:01:38,233 --> 00:01:42,044
Two half arrows, called fish
hooks, each carry one electron.

40
00:01:42,444 --> 00:01:44,431
Each chlorine keeps one electron.

41
00:01:44,431 --> 00:01:46,518
We get two chlorine free radicals.

42
00:01:46,518 --> 00:01:49,588
No charges, but each one
is hungry for a partner.

43
00:01:49,988 --> 00:01:53,739
And if a carbon keeps the pair
instead, it gets a minus charge.

44
00:01:53,739 --> 00:01:57,165
That is a carbanion, with eight
electrons and a lone pair.

45
00:01:57,165 --> 00:02:00,240
It is pyramid shaped, and
its carbon is s p three.

46
00:02:00,890 --> 00:02:03,094
Now, how stable are these pieces?

47
00:02:03,094 --> 00:02:04,102
Step by step.

48
00:02:04,452 --> 00:02:05,352
Step one.

49
00:02:05,139 --> 00:02:09,015
A methyl group, CH three, gently
pushes electrons towards the carbon it

50
00:02:09,015 --> 00:02:09,920
is joined to.

51
00:02:10,670 --> 00:02:11,570
Step two.

52
00:02:11,366 --> 00:02:13,413
A carbocation is short of electrons.

53
00:02:13,413 --> 00:02:17,353
Each methyl group around it feeds it
a little, so more methyl groups make

54
00:02:17,353 --> 00:02:18,356
it more stable.

55
00:02:19,106 --> 00:02:20,012
Step three.

56
00:02:20,012 --> 00:02:24,554
So the order is methyl, then primary,
then secondary, then tertiary.

57
00:02:24,554 --> 00:02:27,126
Tertiary is the most stable carbocation.

58
00:02:27,126 --> 00:02:29,500
Free radicals follow the same order.

59
00:02:30,250 --> 00:02:31,150
Step four.

60
00:02:30,947 --> 00:02:33,206
A carbanion already
has too many electrons.

61
00:02:33,206 --> 00:02:36,578
Extra pushing makes it worse, so
for carbanions the order flips.

62
00:02:36,578 --> 00:02:38,060
Methyl is the most stable.

63
00:02:38,810 --> 00:02:40,344
Now meet the attackers.

64
00:02:40,344 --> 00:02:42,448
A nucleophile means nucleus lover.

65
00:02:42,448 --> 00:02:47,216
It has a spare pair of electrons to
give, like a friend holding extra sweets.

66
00:02:47,566 --> 00:02:50,219
Hydroxide, cyanide, and
carbanions are nucleophiles.

67
00:02:50,219 --> 00:02:53,059
So are water and amines,
because they have lone pairs.

68
00:02:53,409 --> 00:02:55,572
An electrophile means electron lover.

69
00:02:55,572 --> 00:03:00,031
It is short of electrons and wants a
pair, like a friend with empty hands.

70
00:03:00,381 --> 00:03:01,928
Carbocations are electrophiles.

71
00:03:01,928 --> 00:03:05,850
So is the carbon of a CO double bond,
and the carbon in an alkyl halide.

72
00:03:06,250 --> 00:03:07,150
Watch.

73
00:03:06,856 --> 00:03:11,471
The curved arrow starts at the lone
pair of hydroxide and ends on the plus

74
00:03:11,471 --> 00:03:12,371
carbon.

75
00:03:12,126 --> 00:03:13,456
A new bond forms.

76
00:03:13,856 --> 00:03:15,685
A nucleophile gives the pair.

77
00:03:15,685 --> 00:03:17,276
An electrophile takes it.

78
00:03:17,276 --> 00:03:20,275
The curved arrow always
shows the electrons moving.

79
00:03:20,925 --> 00:03:22,294
Compare them side by side.

80
00:03:22,294 --> 00:03:24,189
One brings electrons, one wants them.

81
00:03:24,539 --> 00:03:25,638
Pause and think.

82
00:03:25,638 --> 00:03:28,854
A carbocation has a plus
charge and only six electrons.

83
00:03:28,854 --> 00:03:31,328
Is it a nucleophile, or an electrophile?

84
00:03:34,528 --> 00:03:35,428
An electrophile!

85
00:03:35,407 --> 00:03:38,004
It is short of a pair, so
it looks for electrons.

86
00:03:38,004 --> 00:03:39,190
Nucleophiles attack it.

87
00:03:39,840 --> 00:03:40,740
Remember this.

88
00:03:40,726 --> 00:03:42,290
Arrows follow the electrons.

89
00:03:42,290 --> 00:03:44,656
A nucleophile gives,
an electrophile takes.

90
00:03:45,356 --> 00:03:47,046
Now, one worked example.

91
00:03:47,346 --> 00:03:50,074
Sort these into nucleophiles
and electrophiles.

92
00:03:50,074 --> 00:03:53,937
Hydroxide ion, water, the methyl
carbocation, and cyanide ion.

93
00:03:54,437 --> 00:03:57,736
Hydroxide has lone pairs
and a minus charge.

94
00:03:57,736 --> 00:03:59,188
It gives a pair.

95
00:03:59,188 --> 00:04:00,213
Nucleophile.

96
00:04:00,963 --> 00:04:05,063
Water has no charge, but its
oxygen has two lone pairs.

97
00:04:05,063 --> 00:04:06,983
It can give a pair too.

98
00:04:06,983 --> 00:04:07,975
Nucleophile.

99
00:04:08,725 --> 00:04:12,653
The methyl carbocation has a plus
charge and only six electrons.

100
00:04:12,653 --> 00:04:13,885
It wants a pair.

101
00:04:13,885 --> 00:04:14,803
Electrophile.

102
00:04:15,553 --> 00:04:18,145
Cyanide has a minus
charge and a lone pair.

103
00:04:18,145 --> 00:04:19,045
Nucleophile.

104
00:04:18,948 --> 00:04:21,382
So three nucleophiles
and one electrophile.

105
00:04:22,032 --> 00:04:23,493
Now, the exam trap.

106
00:04:23,843 --> 00:04:27,637
Some students draw the curved arrow
from the plus carbon to the hydroxide

107
00:04:27,637 --> 00:04:30,474
ion, as if the carbon
goes to get the electrons.

108
00:04:30,874 --> 00:04:31,774
Wrong.

109
00:04:31,430 --> 00:04:35,941
A curved arrow always starts at electrons,
a lone pair or a bond, and points

110
00:04:35,941 --> 00:04:37,162
to where they go.

111
00:04:37,162 --> 00:04:40,169
So it starts on hydroxide
and ends on the carbon.

112
00:04:40,819 --> 00:04:41,803
Where is this asked?

113
00:04:41,803 --> 00:04:45,014
Questions on types of bond fission,
stability order of carbocations, carbanions

114
00:04:45,014 --> 00:04:47,643
and free radicals, and spotting
nucleophiles and electrophiles.

115
00:04:48,243 --> 00:04:49,499
Exam level now.

116
00:04:49,499 --> 00:04:52,767
Which order of carbocation
stability is correct?

117
00:04:52,767 --> 00:04:54,077
Pause and solve.

118
00:04:59,477 --> 00:05:00,883
The answer is B.

119
00:05:01,383 --> 00:05:04,681
More methyl groups around the plus
carbon push in more electrons.

120
00:05:05,181 --> 00:05:10,187
The methyl carbocation has none,
primary has one carbon group, secondary

121
00:05:10,187 --> 00:05:11,886
two, tertiary three.

122
00:05:12,386 --> 00:05:15,647
So the stability rises
from methyl to tertiary.

123
00:05:15,647 --> 00:05:19,247
Option D is the carbanion
order, a common mix up.

124
00:05:19,847 --> 00:05:21,388
Here is your recap.

125
00:05:21,388 --> 00:05:22,972
Screenshot this card.

126
00:05:26,172 --> 00:05:28,358
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

127
00:05:28,758 --> 00:05:29,658
One.

128
00:05:29,122 --> 00:05:32,004
What is the difference between
homolytic and heterolytic fission?

129
00:05:32,804 --> 00:05:33,704
Two.

130
00:05:33,325 --> 00:05:37,373
Why is a tertiary carbocation
more stable than a primary one?

131
00:05:38,173 --> 00:05:39,073
Three.

132
00:05:38,779 --> 00:05:42,195
Is water a nucleophile or
an electrophile, and why?

133
00:05:43,095 --> 00:05:46,440
Next time: how can one chlorine atom
pull electrons all along a carbon

134
00:05:46,440 --> 00:05:47,340
chain?

135
00:05:46,900 --> 00:05:49,339
We find out in inductive
and electromeric effects.
